WEBVTT

00:00.465 --> 00:05.097
You're a door-to-door salesman for a
summer, and you have just made your second

00:05.132 --> 00:09.395
sale of the day. There's still
time to knock on one more door.

00:09.935 --> 00:14.065
If two sales is what you normally
make, you might call it a day.

00:14.545 --> 00:18.985
If you usually make three,
two can feel like coming up short.

00:19.395 --> 00:23.335
The question is would you
still knock that last door?

00:24.045 --> 00:29.397
This is the subject of a new paper by
Samuel Dodini aimed at understanding why

00:29.432 --> 00:34.627
people behave this way with mental
targets in mind. Is it a cognitive error?

00:34.662 --> 00:39.517
A bad habit? Or could it be
a rational response to other

00:39.552 --> 00:41.215
self control issues?

00:42.065 --> 00:47.407
People often fall short of long-run goals
because it's easier sometimes to tell

00:47.442 --> 00:52.455
ourselves we'll try harder tomorrow
instead of putting in the effort today.

00:53.055 --> 00:57.207
But let's say that the company you work
for has put a bonus at the end of the

00:57.242 --> 01:02.537
summer for hitting a certain number of
sales. At the pest control sales company

01:02.572 --> 01:07.687
in this study, reaching a hundred and
fifty sales meant qualifying for a big

01:07.722 --> 01:12.325
cash bonus. There's no bonus
for hitting a Tuesday target,

01:12.375 --> 01:16.367
but if you want to stay on pace for
August, you might give yourself one

01:16.402 --> 01:20.877
anyway. Can a reward
months away change when you

01:20.912 --> 01:22.265
go home today?

01:23.115 --> 01:27.477
Dodini studied app and sales records
from more than five hundred door-to-door

01:27.512 --> 01:32.995
sellers over two summers. The records
show when they pitched, when they sold,

01:33.155 --> 01:37.877
and when they knocked on their last door.
To estimate what each seller expected on a

01:37.912 --> 01:42.155
Tuesday, he used their sales on
previous Tuesdays that season.

01:42.515 --> 01:46.445
He then compared when sellers stopped
on either side of that benchmark,

01:46.495 --> 01:49.485
accounting for things like
time on shift and weather.

01:50.105 --> 01:55.457
Once sellers reached their benchmark,
each additional sale was associated with a

01:55.492 --> 01:59.095
steeper rise in the chance
of stopping work for the day,

01:59.385 --> 02:04.907
even if that happened with plenty of time
left to keep working. In other words,

02:04.942 --> 02:08.045
they chose not to knock that last door.

02:08.955 --> 02:13.957
Something similar happened near the end of
the summer. After crossing the threshold

02:13.992 --> 02:18.667
they were aiming for at the end of the
summer, sellers worked about an hour and a

02:18.702 --> 02:24.127
half less per day, even though there
were still commissions on their sales and

02:24.162 --> 02:27.205
there was still time left
in the season to work.

02:28.095 --> 02:32.215
The company used the same season-end
bonus schedule for all its sellers.

02:32.775 --> 02:35.767
To see what the bonus itself
could causally change,

02:35.802 --> 02:40.257
Dodini performed an online experiment in
which people were paid to press buttons

02:40.292 --> 02:44.725
online. He randomly assigned
participants to different pay rules.

02:44.995 --> 02:50.017
The task was simple. Alternate the A
and B keys to score points across four

02:50.052 --> 02:54.375
rounds. One group earned a little
money for every hundred points.

02:54.645 --> 02:58.217
Two other groups could earn a dollar
by reaching either two thousand or

02:58.252 --> 03:00.525
twenty-four hundred points in total.

03:01.095 --> 03:05.055
For the two-thousand-point group,
five hundred a round would do it.

03:05.415 --> 03:10.107
Only the final total determined the bonus,
so people could fall behind and catch up

03:10.142 --> 03:15.487
later. Yet in the early rounds, scores
clustered just above five hundred more

03:15.522 --> 03:19.827
under the bonus than under pay per
point. With a higher bonus target,

03:19.862 --> 03:24.387
the clusters shifted higher. Some
participants said they deliberately aimed

03:24.422 --> 03:27.545
above pace to leave room
for fatigue later on.

03:28.105 --> 03:32.685
This behavior of mental targeting is what
economists call reference dependence.

03:32.915 --> 03:36.887
The same score can feel like a success or
a shortfall depending on the mark you’re

03:36.922 --> 03:42.015
aiming for. Coming up one short can sting
more than going one over feels good.

03:42.225 --> 03:46.807
That’s loss aversion. In the experiment,
the two-thousand-point bonus produced

03:46.842 --> 03:50.867
similar average scores with thirty-one
percent lower incentive payments than pay

03:50.902 --> 03:55.647
per point. Both the door-to-door sales
setting and the online experiment show how

03:55.682 --> 04:00.297
powerful these internal incentives can
be and how people use one behavioral bias

04:00.332 --> 04:03.495
like loss aversion to help
fix self-control problems.

04:04.145 --> 04:09.755
For the full study, read Samuel Dodini’s
“Making Reference-Dependent Preferences:

04:09.845 --> 04:14.045
Evidence from Door-to-Door Sales,”
published in Labour Economics.

04:14.265 --> 04:16.025
You’ll find the link on screen.
